{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BoAPMC1fAGmaHc37FhWTga","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted data for all planned experiments in the experiment_plan, based on the \"best\" tier definition. Let me analyze the experiment plan and registry to understand what experiments are planned and what results to predict.\n\nLet me first review what's in the experiment plan and what's already done vs planned, and what the \"best\" tier means:\n\n**Best tier definition:**\n- C1 ablation cross-condition validation + BCE@30% significantly worse than KL + regime boundaries clear\n- Mask pattern discovery narrative fully holds\n- Core hypotheses:\n  1. C1 ablation (BCE→KL targets) uniform allocation: oracle agreement ≥ 0.95, PPL gap vs KL < 5%\n  2. Standard BCE@30% TEAL PPL ≥ 12.0\n  3. Mask divergence at 30% sparsity ≥ 40%\n  4. 25%/35% sweep confirms 30% allocation preference transition (uniform→global crossover)\n\nNow I'm looking at the expected metric ranges for these experiments—the C1 ablation uniform PPL should fall between 8.5 and 9.5, oracle agreement between 0.95 and 0.99, BCE@30% TEAL PPL between 12.0 and 20.0, and KL@30% uniform MMLU mean around 0.39.\n\nFor the planned experiments, I have three main evaluations queued up: LongBench English subset, MT-Bench, and RULER synthetic long-context testing. I'm also tracking the C1 ablation BCE/KL targets experiment which is partially complete, and there's a dense sparsity sweep still in progress.\n\nLooking at what's currently running, I see BCE at 30% is active, while the KL sparsity variants at 25% and 35% are preparing, along with the C1 ablation uniform evaluation. I need to generate predictions across all these planned experiments using the best tier data I have, focusing on the C1 ablation results I already collected and the uniform allocation evaluation that's queued up.\n\nFor the C1 ablation with uniform allocation, the existing S42 data showed PPL of 9.29 at around 60% sparsity, which is higher than the target. When I apply uniform allocation at a matched sparsity level around 50%, I'd expect the BCE-trained model on KL-derived masks to perform comparably to pure KL, with the oracle agreement metric providing insight into how well the learned masks align with the optimal ones.\n\nFor BCE at 30% sparsity, the best tier baseline suggests PPL should be at least 12.0. Looking at the existing BCE result at 50% sparsity which gave PPL around 28, I'd expect improvement at 30% but still significantly worse than KL's performance at the same sparsity level. Given that KL at 30% achieves PPL around 8.8-9.0, I'm predicting BCE at 30% will land in the 14-15 range.\n\nFor KL at 25% sparsity, I'm checking what the best tier expects for this configuration.\n\nFor KL at 35% sparsity, the best tier predicts PPL between 8.8 and 9.5. Looking at the pattern: KL at 30% gives PPL around 8.82, but interestingly KL at 40% actually gives a lower PPL of around 8.36. This non-monotonic behavior is what the paper mentioned—more sparsity doesn't always mean worse performance. So at 35%, I'd expect the PPL to fall somewhere between those two values, probably around 8.5-8.7. likely ~8.6-8.7, but the best tier predicts [8.8, 9.5] for 35% which seems high. Let me think through this more carefully—the non-monotonicity in the data (30% uniform PPL = 8.82 vs 40% = 8.36) suggests there's a transition zone around 30-35% where the allocation preference shifts and the model starts losing capacity more steeply. The 25%/35% sweep appears to be tracking this crossover point where global allocation becomes competitive with uniform, which would explain why 35% uniform PPL jumps up compared to 30%. I'm realizing the PPL range might reflect a different training setup—the 35% model uses a dedicated KL predictor trained at that sparsity level, which could yield different results than just evaluating the 30% model at 35% allocation. So training a new predictor targeting 35% sparsity with uniform allocation would likely give PPL around 8.5-9.0, which aligns better with the expected range.\n\nBased on this logic, I'd estimate 25% uniform PPL around 7.9 and 35% uniform PPL around 9.1. Now I'm looking at the LongBench results—I have dense baseline metrics and KL-30% uniform results across several tasks, so I can compare the performance gaps.\n\nThe multi_news discrepancy between dense (2.5) and KL30 (18.01) stands out as potentially using a different evaluation metric. For the best tier, I'd expect KL to show moderate degradation on long-context tasks while still maintaining its advantage over TEAL.\n\nI'm also considering MT-Bench for multi-turn instruction following, where the best tier should show KL keeping coherent responses close to dense performance, and RULER for synthetic long-context diagnostics to check retrieval at 4K/16K lengths. Looking at the mask divergence analysis, the 50% sparsity showed 43.3% divergence, so at 30% sparsity I'd predict the divergence stays above 40%.\n\nNow I'm organizing the YAML output by listing all experiments to predict: the running and preparing ones from the registry (BCE 30%, KL sparsity variants, and the ablation eval), the planned benchmarks (LongBench, MT-Bench, RULER), and the what_if experiments that overlap with the ablation evaluations. I need to map out predictions for all the planned experiments: LongBench, MT-Bench, and RULER benchmarks, plus the ablation and sparsity variants currently running. The key validation points are whether the C1 ablation maintains oracle agreement above 0.95 with less than 5% PPL degradation, and confirming that KL sparsity substantially outperforms BCE at the 30% compression level.\n\nFor the mask divergence metric at 30%, I don't have an explicit experiment planned yet, but I can derive it from the existing KL predictors. Let me structure predictions for each experiment systematically, starting with what the C1 ablation uniform allocation tells me—the BCE predictor trained on KL-derived masks hit 9.29 PPL at 60.9% sparsity, which gives me a baseline for comparison.\n\nNow looking at the oracle agreement of 0.978 and thinking through the uniform allocation scenario: if I evaluate this predictor at 50% uniform sparsity instead of the 60.9% it was trained on, the performance should shift. The reference KL baseline at 50% uniform is 11.96 PPL, so a BCE predictor trained on KL targets should land somewhere around 9.0 PPL—though I need to reconcile whether that's actually better than KL or if the effective sparsity difference is creating an unfair comparison. The \"PPL gap vs KL < 5%\" constraint suggests the predictor should stay close to KL quality at equivalent sparsity levels. I'm realizing the comparison is actually apples-to-apples now—both are measuring hard-mask PPL, and the C1 ablation at 60.9% sparsity is achieving 9.29, which is comparable to KL's 9.2 at 50% sparsity, meaning the ablation is actually more aggressive while maintaining similar performance.\n\nFor the uniform allocation variant, I'm trying to figure out what metric the c1_ablation_uniform_ppl is actually measuring—it could be per-token hard mask evaluation at 50% uniform sparsity, which would land around 9.0-9.5, similar to where KL's per-token hard mask PPL sits.\n\nRather than overthinking the exact breakdown, I should just produce predictions that align with the best tier ranges and move forward with writing out the full YAML with clear reasoning for each metric.\n\nFor c1_ablation_eval_uniform, I'm predicting per-token PPL around 9.1 since BCE on KL-oracle masks should capture the same structure, and uniform allocation removes layer imbalance effects that would otherwise hurt performance. For bce_30pct_s42, I'm expecting PPL around 14.5—BCE's factorized approach still can't capture cross-layer interactions like KL does, though the gap narrows somewhat at 30% sparsity compared to 50%.\n\nAt 25% sparsity with KL, uniform allocation keeps most layers above 75% capacity, yielding PPL around 7.9 and MMLU scores around 0.45, while global top-k introduces mild imbalance that pushes performance down slightly. At 35% sparsity, the budget constraints become tighter, pushing PPL toward 9.1 and MMLU scores lower as the method approaches a critical transition point where uniform allocation's benefits start diminishing.\n\nNow I'm mapping out expected performance across different benchmarks—LongBench should show KL 30% uniform retaining 55-65% of dense performance while TEAL degrades further, MT-Bench expects a clear hierarchy from dense down through KL 50%, and RULER will reveal how well KL maintains capabilities at longer sequence lengths like 4K and 16K.\n\nFor the mask divergence at 30% sparsity, I need to run a separate overlap analysis. Looking back at the experimental structure, I can see how the ablation studies, sparsity sweeps, and forced top-k experiments map to the existing results I have, so I'll organize the YAML to capture all these predictions and their relationships. I need to predict results for all the planned experiments and include the mask divergence analysis at 30% since it's a key metric. Let me structure the YAML output with the proper format: tier, narrative guidance, and imagined experiments with their expected results, rationales, and analysis sections. I'll calibrate against the Llama 3.1 8B baseline data I have available. Looking at the performance metrics across different KL divergence configurations and TEAL approaches, I'm seeing that the 40% uniform variant achieves strong results with a lower perplexity of 8.36, while TEAL 30% shows competitive performance at 10.97 perplexity with the highest ARC-c score of 0.423.\n\nThe BCE 50% global configuration shows significant degradation with much higher perplexity at 27.97, suggesting this approach struggles with the iso-compute constraint. For the C1 ablation, I'm examining the mask overlap statistics—the 39.6% Jaccard similarity with 43.3% divergence at 50% sparsity indicates substantial differences in which tokens the predictor selects compared to the oracle, and now I'm moving into evaluating how the C1 ablation predictor performs when applied uniformly across the evaluation set.\n\nThe predictor achieved near-perfect oracle agreement at 0.978, so under uniform allocation it should closely match KL's 50% uniform performance of 11.96 TEAL PPL. However, the reported C1 ablation results showing PPL around 8.5-9.5 don't align with this—they're significantly better, which suggests these numbers might be measuring something different, possibly the hard mask PPL at the predictor's actual 60.9% sparsity rather than the target 50%.\n\nLooking at the best tier narrative, the core finding is that KL discovers masks fundamentally different from magnitude heuristics (>50% divergence), and BCE trained on these KL targets fully recovers the quality, proving that the masks themselves matter more than the training signal. I should structure my predictions around confirming this central claim while accounting for the different evaluation metrics at play.\n\nNow I'm compiling the multi-seed statistics from the 3-seed KL 30% uniform runs to establish baseline performance across different random seeds, which will help ground my predictions for the ablation results. I'm noticing the MMLU variance across seeds is quite high—s42 shows 0.406 globally but only 0.287 in one context, while s123 and s456 cluster around 0.373-0.376. Since I'm predicting values for experiments not yet run rather than analyzing existing data, I should focus on the what-if scenarios and also include mask_divergence_30pct as a new metric to analyze, particularly since the 53% headline mask divergence builds on the 43.3% value at 50%. The 53% divergence at 30% sparsity makes sense because at lower sparsity levels, the marginal neurons become more ambiguous—KL's joint optimization finds different trade-offs than magnitude-based ranking, whereas at 50% sparsity the obvious high-magnitude neurons create more overlap between the two methods.\n\nNow I'm structuring out the experimental predictions: I'll set up eight different experiment configurations covering ablation studies, sparsity variations, divergence analysis, and benchmark evaluations, then work through detailed predictions for each one.\n\nFor the uniform allocation ablation, the predictor trained on KL oracle masks should maintain high agreement (~0.97) when forced to 50% uniform sparsity, with per-token perplexity around 9.0-9.2 and TEAL perplexity in the 11.5-12.5 range. At 30% uniform, I'd expect per-token perplexity dropping to 7.2-7.5 and TEAL perplexity around 8.5-9.0.\n\nLooking at the c1_ablation_uniform_ppl range of [8.5, 9.5], this appears to be at 50% sparsity since that's where the c1 ablation was trained. The range suggests c1 could actually perform slightly better than KL's ~9.2 per-token perplexity at 50%, possibly due to higher effective sparsity during training, so I'll set the per-token PPL estimate at 9.1 with oracle agreement staying at 0.978 and MMLU matching KL's uniform performance at around 0.28.\n\nNow for the BCE predictor at 30% sparsity—since it's trained on KL targets, it might generalize better under uniform allocation than the per-neuron factorized approach typically allows. I'm predicting c1_ablation_uniform_ppl at 9.05 with a slight improvement of -1.6% gap versus KL. For bce_30pct_s42, I need to evaluate standard BCE at 30% sparsity, which should perform better than at 50% due to less aggressive pruning, but the per-neuron factorization still limits its ability to capture cross-layer patterns—so it should fall between TEAL's 8.98 and vanilla's 10.97 at that sparsity level.\n\nLooking at the actual results though, I'm noticing something odd: BCE at 50% made things worse (27.97 vs vanilla TEAL's 23.31), and the isocompute version was even worse at 54.84. This suggests the BCE predictor isn't actually helping when applied through TEAL, despite the different allocation strategies involved. So at 30% sparsity, BCE might perform relatively better than at 50% since there's more budget, but it's still fundamentally limited by its per-neuron factorized loss approach. I'm predicting BCE at 30% would land around 14.2 PPL, which would actually be worse than vanilla TEAL's 10.97, suggesting KL's approach is substantially more effective at this sparsity level.\n\nLooking at the 50% pattern where BCE was about 1.2× worse than vanilla TEAL, if that ratio holds at 30%, I'd get around 13.2 PPL. But the ratio might shift at lower sparsity—BCE could relatively improve since fewer cross-layer interactions mean the factorized loss is less problematic, so maybe the degradation factor would be smaller.\n\nNow examining the KL predictor results at 25% sparsity. The PPL improves as we move from 30% uniform (8.82) toward the denser 25% target, so I'm estimating around 7.9 for the 25% uniform case. For MMLU, the uniform allocation advantage should be even more pronounced at 25% due to the larger budget surplus, suggesting the score would improve from the 30% uniform value of 0.406.\n\nLooking at the downstream task performance at 25% sparsity across ARC-c, WG, MMLU, and GSM8K, the scores show consistent improvements over the 30% uniform baseline. At 25% global allocation, MMLU performance gets closer to uniform but remains slightly worse, indicating the uniform advantage persists but diminishes with the more generous budget.\n\nFor the 35% sparsity case, I'm interpolating between the 30% uniform PPL of 8.82 and the 40% uniform PPL of 8.36 to estimate where 35% should fall.  The non-monotonicity at 40% might be specific to that predictor, so at 35% I'd expect around 9.1 PPL, which fits within the tier range. For downstream MMLU at 35%, I'm expecting performance to fall in the transition region between 30% and 40%, with global allocation starting to narrow the gap with uniform.\n\nLooking at the broader pattern, 35% appears to be the crossover point where uniform's advantage over global shrinks from double digits down to just a couple percentage points. At 30% sparsity, the KL versus TEAL mask overlap divergence ratio sits around 0.433, and I'm expecting something in the 0.4 to 0.6 range at this level.\n\nNow examining the LongBench results—I have dense baseline numbers and KL 30% uniform performance across narrativeqa, qasper, and gov_report tasks, showing retention rates hovering around 55-65%. I need to fill in predictions for TEAL at 30% and other conditions to complete the comparison.\n\nFor MT-Bench, I'm estimating performance across pruning methods and sparsity levels, starting with the dense baseline around 6.8 and working through KL and TEAL variants at different retention rates.\n\nNow moving to RULER synthetic benchmarks at 4K and 16K context lengths, where I expect accuracy to degrade more sharply at higher sparsity levels, particularly for the longer sequences.\n\nI have enough predictions across all three benchmarks to construct a coherent narrative, so let me verify the consistency between the ablation findings and these performance projections before writing out the full YAML specification.\n\nThe story is holding together: KL divergence uncovers fundamentally different masking patterns that BCE can't discover on its own, the oracle agreement validates that masks are the primary driver, and the regime analysis shows where the crossover happens between uniform and global strategies. I should also plan out the tables and figures—main results table, regime analysis breakdown across sparsity levels, and supporting ablations.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the experimental setup more carefully. Some runs are single-seed while others have multiple seeds, and I need to be consistent about what's feasible for the sweep experiments. Also reconsidering whether the c1_ablation at 30% sparsity is necessary given that the what_if experiment is already defined at 50% to match the original training setup.\n\nLooking at the mask_divergence_30pct analysis separately—that uses the kl_sparsity30_s42_v3 predictor at 30% sparsity, which is distinct from the other experiments. Now I have a clearer picture of how these fit together. I should write out the comprehensive YAML structure, keeping the analysis sections focused on what each result contributes to the paper's narrative and which tables they feed into. I'm mapping out the remaining evaluation experiments—LongBench for long-context performance, MT-Bench for generation quality, and RULER for synthetic diagnostics—each tied to their respective plan IDs and result tables. Now I'm ready to structure this into the final YAML configuration.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34240,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":34240},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e3aa115-73c0-4d5f-be4a-068fa30e0ac6","uuid":"2d335025-58a2-4e68-a50c-f4f17470f5be"}
